From 431dbe8d647cb1692ad718195dfbdfbb1a74a9af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VoidChecksum <markuskvam28b@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 21:39:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] accel/rocket: look up core clocks by name

The clk_bulk_data array passed to devm_clk_bulk_get() never has its
.id fields set, so every element resolves through clk_get(dev, NULL),
which on DT platforms returns the first clock of the node. The driver
ends up with four references to "aclk" and never touches hclk, npu or
pclk.

This has gone unnoticed on RK3588 because the clocks the cores need are
kept running by other means there (the NPU power domains list them, and
the "npu" clock is an SCMI clock managed by firmware), but it breaks on
SoCs where the core actually depends on the driver enabling its own
bus clocks, and it makes the clk_bulk PM callbacks operate on the wrong
clock either way.

The binding has always required clock-names = "aclk", "hclk", "npu",
"pclk", so look the clocks up by name.

Jiaxing Hu tracked this down during RK3576 bring-up, where probing the
core hangs with hclk gated.

Fixes: ed98261b4168 ("accel/rocket: Add a new driver for Rockchip's NPU")
Reported-by: Jiaxing Hu <huhuvmb88@gmail.com>
Closes: https://github.com/flipperdevices/flipperone-linux-build-scripts/issues/55
Signed-off-by: VoidChecksum <markuskvam28b@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.c
index b3b2fa9ba..5dd260bac 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.c
@@ -28,6 +28,10 @@ int rocket_core_init(struct rocket_core *core)
 	if (err)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, err, "failed to get resets for core %d\n", core->index);
 
+	core->clks[0].id = "aclk";
+	core->clks[1].id = "hclk";
+	core->clks[2].id = "npu";
+	core->clks[3].id = "pclk";
 	err = devm_clk_bulk_get(dev, ARRAY_SIZE(core->clks), core->clks);
 	if (err)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, err, "failed to get clocks for core %d\n", core->index);
-- 
